<Layout 
  title="Physics Glossary | Key Terms & Definitions for IB, A-Level, GCSE, AP"
  description="Comprehensive glossary of essential physics terms and definitions. From classical mechanics to quantum physics, find clear explanations of key concepts used in IB, A-Level, GCSE, AP, and university-level physics."
>
  <section class="hero">
    <h1>Physics<br><span>Glossary of Key Terms</span></h1>
    <p>Alphabetically sorted definitions of essential physics concepts, from classical mechanics to particle physics.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="section-header">
      <h2>Definitions</h2>
    </div>

    <dl>
      <dt>Acceleration</dt>
      <dd>The rate of change of velocity with respect to time. Vector quantity measured in m/s². See <a href="https://physics.wyattau.com/docs/physics/1-classical-mechanics">Classical Mechanics</a>.</dd>

      <dt>Angular Momentum</dt>
      <dd>The rotational analog of linear momentum: L = r × p. Conserved when no external torque acts on a system. See <a href="https://physics.wyattau.com/docs/physics/1-classical-mechanics">Classical Mechanics</a>.</dd>

      <dt>Atomic Number</dt>
      <dd>The number of protons in an atom's nucleus, defining the element. Denoted Z. Determines electron configuration and chemical properties.</dd>

      <dt>Blackbody Radiation</dt>
      <dd>Electromagnetic radiation emitted by a perfect absorber in thermal equilibrium. Spectral distribution depends only on temperature. Led to quantum mechanics. See <a href="https://physics.wyattau.com/docs/physics/5-quantum-mechanics">Quantum Mechanics</a>.</dd>

      <dt>Capacitance</dt>
      <dd>The ability of a system to store charge per unit voltage: C = Q/V. Measured in farads (F). See <a href="https://physics.wyattau.com/docs/physics/3-electromagnetism">Electromagnetism</a>.</dd>

      <dt>Centrifugal Force</dt>
      <dd>An apparent outward force experienced in a rotating reference frame. A fictitious force arising from non-inertial frame effects.</dd>

      <dt>Conservation of Energy</dt>
      <dd>The principle that total energy in an isolated system remains constant. Energy can transform between forms but cannot be created or destroyed.</dd>

      <dt>Conservation of Momentum</dt>
      <dd>The total momentum of an isolated system remains constant. Applies to both linear and angular momentum in the absence of external forces.</dd>

      <dt>Current Density</dt>
      <dd>Current per unit cross-sectional area: J = I/A. Vector quantity describing the flow of electric charge. See <a href="https://physics.wyattau.com/docs/physics/3-electromagnetism">Electromagnetism</a>.</dd>

      <dt>Dielectric Constant</dt>
      <dd>The ratio of a material's permittivity to the permittivity of free space. Describes how a material affects the electric field within it.</dd>

      <dt>Diffraction</dt>
      <dd>The bending of waves around obstacles and through openings. Significant when wavelength is comparable to obstacle size. See <a href="https://physics.wyattau.com/docs/physics/4-optics-and-waves">Optics & Waves</a>.</dd>

      <dt>Electric Field</dt>
      <dd>A vector field representing the force per unit charge at each point. Created by charges and changing magnetic fields. See <a href="https://physics.wyattau.com/docs/physics/3-electromagnetism">Electromagnetism</a>.</dd>

      <dt>Electromagnetic Spectrum</dt>
      <dd>The range of all electromagnetic radiation frequencies. From radio waves (long wavelength) to gamma rays (short wavelength).</dd>

      <dt>Entropy</dt>
      <dd>A measure of disorder or the number of microscopic configurations corresponding to a thermodynamic state. Always increases in isolated systems. See <a href="https://physics.wyattau.com/docs/physics/2-thermal-physics">Thermal Physics</a>.</dd>

      <dt>Faraday's Law</dt>
      <dd>A changing magnetic flux induces an electromotive force (EMF) proportional to the rate of change: ε = −dΦ/dt. See <a href="https://physics.wyattau.com/docs/physics/3-electromagnetism">Electromagnetism</a>.</dd>

      <dt>Force</dt>
      <dd>An interaction that changes the motion of an object. Measured in newtons (N). F = ma in classical mechanics. See <a href="https://physics.wyattau.com/docs/physics/1-classical-mechanics">Classical Mechanics</a>.</dd>

      <dt>Friction</dt>
      <dd>A force opposing relative motion between surfaces. Static friction prevents motion; kinetic friction opposes sliding motion.</dd>

      <dt>Gauge Boson</dt>
      <dd>A force-carrying particle mediating fundamental interactions. Includes photons (EM), W/Z bosons (weak), and gluons (strong). See <a href="https://physics.wyattau.com/docs/physics/7-particle-physics-and-cosmology">Particle Physics</a>.</dd>

      <dt>Gravitational Field</dt>
      <dd>The region of space around a mass where gravitational force is experienced. Strength decreases with the square of distance. See <a href="https://physics.wyattau.com/docs/physics/1-classical-mechanics">Classical Mechanics</a>.</dd>

      <dt>Half-Life</dt>
      <dd>The time for half the nuclei in a radioactive sample to decay. Constant for a given isotope. See <a href="https://physics.wyattau.com/docs/physics/7-particle-physics-and-cosmology">Particle Physics</a>.</dd>

      <dt>Harmonic Oscillator</dt>
      <dd>A system experiencing a restoring force proportional to displacement. Models pendulums, springs, and molecular vibrations. See <a href="https://physics.wyattau.com/docs/physics/1-classical-mechanics">Classical Mechanics</a>.</dd>

      <dt>Impulse</dt>
      <dd>The change in momentum of an object: J = FΔt = Δp. Equal to the integral of force over time. See <a href="https://physics.wyattau.com/docs/physics/1-classical-mechanics">Classical Mechanics</a>.</dd>

      <dt>Interference</dt>
      <dd>The superposition of two or more waves producing a combined amplitude different from individual amplitudes. Constructive and destructive interference patterns. See <a href="https://physics.wyattau.com/docs/physics/4-optics-and-waves">Optics & Waves</a>.</dd>

      <dt>Kinetic Energy</dt>
      <dd>The energy of motion: KE = ½mv² for classical particles. A scalar quantity measured in joules (J). See <a href="https://physics.wyattau.com/docs/physics/1-classical-mechanics">Classical Mechanics</a>.</dd>

      <dt>Lagrangian</dt>
      <dd>A function L = T − V (kinetic minus potential energy) used to derive equations of motion via the principle of least action. See <a href="https://physics.wyattau.com/docs/physics/1-classical-mechanics">Classical Mechanics</a>.</dd>

      <dt>Lorentz Force</dt>
      <dd>The force on a charged particle in electromagnetic fields: F = q(E + v × B). Describes combined electric and magnetic effects. See <a href="https://physics.wyattau.com/docs/physics/3-electromagnetism">Electromagnetism</a>.</dd>

      <dt>Magnetic Flux</dt>
      <dd>The integral of the magnetic field over a surface: Φ = ∫B · dA. Changes in flux induce EMF. See <a href="https://physics.wyattau.com/docs/physics/3-electromagnetism">Electromagnetism</a>.</dd>

      <dt>Mass-Energy Equivalence</dt>
      <dd>Einstein's relation E = mc² showing mass and energy are interchangeable. Explains energy release in nuclear reactions.</dd>

      <dt>Maxwell's Equations</dt>
      <dd>Four equations unifying electricity and magnetism: Gauss's law, Faraday's law, Ampère's law, and Gauss's magnetism law. See <a href="https://physics.wyattau.com/docs/physics/3-electromagnetism">Electromagnetism</a>.</dd>

      <dt>Momentum</dt>
      <dd>The product of mass and velocity: p = mv. A conserved vector quantity in isolated systems. See <a href="https://physics.wyattau.com/docs/physics/1-classical-mechanics">Classical Mechanics</a>.</dd>

      <dt>Ohm's Law</dt>
      <dd>The relationship between voltage, current, and resistance: V = IR. Describes linear behavior of resistors. See <a href="https://physics.wyattau.com/docs/physics/3-electromagnetism">Electromagnetism</a>.</dd>

      <dt>Pauli Exclusion Principle</dt>
      <dd>No two identical fermions can occupy the same quantum state simultaneously. Explains electron shell structure and matter stability. See <a href="https://physics.wyattau.com/docs/physics/5-quantum-mechanics">Quantum Mechanics</a>.</dd>

      <dt>Photoelectric Effect</dt>
      <dd>Emission of electrons when light strikes a material. Energy of emitted electrons depends on frequency, not intensity. Evidence for light quantization. See <a href="https://physics.wyattau.com/docs/physics/5-quantum-mechanics">Quantum Mechanics</a>.</dd>

      <dt>Potential Energy</dt>
      <dd>Energy stored by position or configuration of a system. Gravitational PE = mgh; elastic PE = ½kx². See <a href="https://physics.wyattau.com/docs/physics/1-classical-mechanics">Classical Mechanics</a>.</dd>

      <dt>Quantum Entanglement</dt>
      <dd>A phenomenon where particles share quantum states such that measuring one instantaneously affects the other, regardless of distance.</dd>

      <dt>Resonance</dt>
      <dd>The amplification of oscillations when a driving frequency matches the natural frequency of a system. See <a href="https://physics.wyattau.com/docs/physics/4-optics-and-waves">Optics & Waves</a>.</dd>

      <dt>Snell's Law</dt>
      <dd>The relationship between angles of incidence and refraction: n₁ sin θ₁ = n₂ sin θ₂. Governs light bending at interfaces. See <a href="https://physics.wyattau.com/docs/physics/4-optics-and-waves">Optics & Waves</a>.</dd>

      <dt>Specific Heat Capacity</dt>
      <dd>The energy required to raise the temperature of 1 kg of a substance by 1 K. Material property measured in J/(kg·K). See <a href="https://physics.wyattau.com/docs/physics/2-thermal-physics">Thermal Physics</a>.</dd>

      <dt>Standard Model</dt>
      <dd>The theoretical framework describing fundamental particles (quarks, leptons, bosons) and three of four forces. See <a href="https://physics.wyattau.com/docs/physics/7-particle-physics-and-cosmology">Particle Physics</a>.</dd>

      <dt>Temperature</dt>
      <dd>A measure of average kinetic energy of particles in a system. Related to entropy and energy distribution via statistical mechanics.</dd>

      <dt>Thermodynamics</dt>
      <dd>The study of energy, heat, work, and entropy. Governed by four laws describing energy transformations. See <a href="https://physics.wyattau.com/docs/physics/2-thermal-physics">Thermal Physics</a>.</dd>

      <dt>Torque</dt>
      <dd>The rotational equivalent of force: τ = r × F. Causes angular acceleration. Measured in newton-meters (N·m). See <a href="https://physics.wyattau.com/docs/physics/1-classical-mechanics">Classical Mechanics</a>.</dd>

      <dt>Velocity</dt>
      <dd>The rate of change of displacement with respect to time. A vector quantity with magnitude (speed) and direction. See <a href="https://physics.wyattau.com/docs/physics/1-classical-mechanics">Classical Mechanics</a>.</dd>

      <dt>Wave-Particle Duality</dt>
      <dd>The concept that particles exhibit both wave and particle properties. Fundamental to quantum mechanics. See <a href="https://physics.wyattau.com/docs/physics/5-quantum-mechanics">Quantum Mechanics</a>.</dd>

      <dt>Wavelength</dt>
      <dd>The spatial period of a wave: the distance between consecutive crests. Related to frequency and speed by v = fλ. See <a href="https://physics.wyattau.com/docs/physics/4-optics-and-waves">Optics & Waves</a>.</dd>

      <dt>Work</dt>
      <dd>Energy transferred by a force acting over displacement: W = Fd cos θ. Scalar quantity measured in joules (J). See <a href="https://physics.wyattau.com/docs/physics/1-classical-mechanics">Classical Mechanics</a>.</dd>
    </dl>
  </section>

